  "ai_language_tip": "Speak clear, polite Swedish (sv); almost everyone under 60 speaks good English and switching is accepted, but healthcare (1177/vårdcentral) and authorities prefer Swedish; Finnish is used in some northern areas.",
  "english_ok": "yes — Very high proficiency nationwide; only older staff in rural areas or formal authority lines may prefer Swedish.",
  "politeness": "Informal 'du' is standard even with strangers; open with 'Hej' and say whom you call for; close with 'Tack, hej då'. Polite but brief and to the point.",
  "name_tip": "First name + surname; very informal culture, surname alone is enough for a booking ('i namnet Andersson'); spell foreign names letter by letter, using the Swedish spelling alphabet if needed.",
  "phone_tip": "Mobile 07x-xxx xx xx (10 digits with 0), Stockholm landline 08-xxx xxx xx, +46; Swish payment uses the mobile number.",
  "time_tip": "TRAP: 'halv åtta' = 7:30 (half to eight); 'kvart i åtta' = 7:45; times written with a dot ('kl. 19.30'); read back in 24h.",
  "number_tip": "Read phone numbers in pairs or groups ('noll sju noll...'); 'lgh 1102' is a Swedish flat number (floor 11, flat 02), not a quantity; '3 tr' = third floor.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid lunch 11:30–13:00, dinner peak 18:00–20:00, calls after 19:00 and weekends for offices; July is industrial holiday (semester) when many small businesses close or reduce hours.",
  "closed_days": "Shops open on Sundays (shorter hours); midsommarafton, julafton and nyårsafton close most offices; public holidays (e.g. Ascension, 6 June, Midsommardagen, Alla helgons dag); many small businesses close for weeks in July.",
